# SOLID Principles & Clean Code

## Single Responsibility (SRP)
Each class has ONE reason to change. If a class does multiple things, split it.

```php
// BAD: controller with business logic
public function store(Request $request) {
    $amount = $request->unit_price * $request->quantity + $request->commission;
    $order = SourcingOrder::create([...]);
    Mail::to($order->user)->send(new InvoiceMail($order));
}

// GOOD: controller delegates
public function store(StoreQuotationRequest $request, QuotationService $service) {
    $quotation = $service->create($request->validated());
    return redirect()->route('quotations.show', $quotation);
}
```

## Open/Closed (OCP)
Use interfaces and polymorphism instead of conditionals for extensions.

```php
// GOOD: SheetIntegrationInterface allows new providers without modifying existing code
interface SheetIntegrationInterface {
    public function syncOrder(SourcingOrder $order, ShippingCompany $company): bool;
}
```

## Dependency Inversion (DIP)
Depend on abstractions, inject via constructor.

```php
// GOOD
public function __construct(
    private readonly SourcingOrderRepositoryInterface $orderRepo,
    private readonly OrderStatusServiceInterface $statusService,
) {}
```

## General Rules
- Max 20 lines per method. Extract private methods or delegate to services.
- Max 200 lines per controller. If larger, extract a service.
- No business logic in controllers, models, or listeners.
- No direct `Model::where()` in controllers. Use repositories.
- Prefer early returns over nested if/else.
- Type-hint all method parameters and return types.
- Use `readonly` properties when possible.
- No magic strings: use constants or enums.

## Documentation (PHPDoc / Javadoc-style)
- Add a PHPDoc block to every public class (one-line summary + optional details).
- Add a PHPDoc block to every public and protected method with:
  - One-line summary
  - `@param` for each parameter (type, name, description)
  - `@return` for return type and brief description
  - `@throws` for each exception the method can throw
- See the phpdoc rule for examples.

## Performance
- Eager load relationships to avoid N+1; chunk large result sets.
- Prefer cache for expensive or external calls; queue heavy work (emails, PDFs, APIs).
- Prefer collection methods and early returns; keep methods small.
- See the performance rule for details.
